    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Townville was founded in 1824 by Noah Town, who gave the borough its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Townville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers built sawmills on Muddy Creek’s waterfall, with Noah Town’s 1833 mill among the first.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Townville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sawn lumber was rafted east to Oil Creek, then to the Allegheny River, reaching Pittsburgh.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Townville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the 1859 oil boom, Noah Town supplied oak staves for oil barrels.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Townville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Townville sits in a valley between north‑flowing Muddy Creek and south‑flowing Sugar Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Townville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
